Anorexia and Cachexia
Palliative & End of Life
Unintentional severe weight loss, Profound muscle wasting, Absolute lack of appetite

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-009
Reason for Visit: Profound generalized weakness, syncope, and unremitting nausea completely preventing oral intake.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, daughter
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient suffered a sudden syncopal episode while attempting to walk to the bathroom. The patient is exhibiting profound, debilitating systemic weakness out of proportion to activity, driven by an absolute lack of food intake over the last week due to severe, intractable nausea. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out acute hemorrhagic or cardiac events following the syncope, manage the intractable nausea, and stabilize the patient at home prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I just stood up and the room went black. I have absolutely zero energy, and the thought of food makes me want to heave."
History of Present Illness: Over the past six months, there has been profound, completely unintentional, and continuous loss of total body weight. The daughter reports an absolute lack of physical desire strictly explicitly to eat, coupled with profound early satiety. Over the last 48 hours, chronic, unremitting nausea has severely exacerbated the strict refusal of oral food intake. The patient describes a distinctly altered, frequently metallic or completely absent sense of taste and smell. This has led to progressive, debilitating generalized systemic weakness and fatigue completely out of proportion to any activity, culminating in today's syncopal event when attempting to stand. Sunken facial features distinctly demonstrating profound bitemporal and buccal fat pad wasting are evident. Severe asthenia is directly causing a rapidly declining physical performance status. Mild edema strictly secondary to profound, chronic malnutrition is noted in the ankles.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound following syncope; previously mobilizing short distances with a frame.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk; daughter is highly anxious regarding the complete cessation of oral intake.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/55 mmHg (lying), 70/40 mmHg (standing) | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 36.4 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 42 Kg
General: Profoundly cachectic, frail, pale, but alert and oriented. Appears severely fatigued.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a regular tachycardia with significant postural hypotension drop and profound orthostatic dizziness. Gastrointestinal exam reveals a scaphoid, soft, non-tender abdomen with sluggish bowel sounds. Musculoskeletal exam shows severe, visually striking generalized depletion of skeletal muscle mass and subcutaneous adipose tissue. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the observed unintentional severe weight loss (clothing hanging loosely), profound muscle wasting (visible temporal and thenar atrophy), and the reported absolute lack of appetite with unremitting nausea.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment. Patient is now resting flat in bed, preventing further syncope.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been unable to swallow oral antiemetics (metoclopramide) due to the severe nausea and metallic taste.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Anorexia and Cachexia - Status: Exacerbated (Syncope secondary to profound hypovolemia/malnutrition)
Differentials:

Acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of melena, absence of hematemesis, and stable (though low) baseline pallor without acute diaphoresis or tachycardia out of proportion to the dehydration.

Acute myocardial infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the complete absence of chest pain, shortness of breath, or radiating arm/jaw pain prior to the syncopal event.

Severe sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, absence of a localized infectious source, and clear lung fields/urine.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute syncopal episode directly driven by profound orthostatic hypotension, severe hypovolemia, and asthenia secondary to advanced end-stage anorexia-cachexia syndrome. The objective finding of a profound postural BP drop (85/55 to 70/40) confirms the mechanism of the collapse. Because the unremitting nausea completely prevents oral hydration and medication intake, delaying assessment until regular hours would leave the patient trapped in a cycle of intractable vomiting, worsening dehydration, and highly dangerous, recurrent syncopal falls. Urgent after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to assess the fall, bypass the oral route with parenteral antiemetics, and establish a safe, strict bed-rest plan.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous haloperidol for the intractable, centrally-driven nausea. Instructed strict bed rest with bedside commode use only, due to the profound postural hypotension.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the intense wave of nausea building (trigger), immediately inform your daughter so she can place the cool cloth on your neck and give the under-the-tongue anti-nausea wafer (behavior), which will stop the nausea from triggering actual vomiting (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community dietetics for advice on high-yield, low-volume nutritional supplements if nausea resolves.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bedside commode explicitly required to prevent standing and further syncope.
Follow-up: GP review via telehealth in 24 hours to monitor nausea control.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient experiences a loss of consciousness while lying flat in bed, develops sudden, severe abdominal pain, or vomits fresh blood or "coffee-ground" material, the daughter must call 000 immediately for emergency transport.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The extreme weight loss and lack of appetite are systemic effects of the underlying disease process, not simply a failure to eat.
Clinical Course: Forced feeding will not reverse the cachexia and may cause severe distress or aspiration. The focus must be exclusively on treating the nausea and maintaining comfort.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to stand up under any circumstances until the dizziness resolves. Use the commode strictly with assistance. Focus on tiny sips of clear fluids rather than forcing meals.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The illness changes your metabolism, making food taste bad and causing nausea, which leads to this severe weakness and fainting. The injection will turn off the nausea switch in your brain so you can rest comfortably without retching.
Actionable Steps: Accept that the body currently cannot process large meals. Focus on mouth comfort with ice chips or very small sips of favorite clear fluids when the nausea settles.

